Describe a person you disliked at first but ended up being friends with
- who he/she is
- how you knew him/her
- why you disliked him/her at first but you changed your mind
- explain how you feel about the experience

One person who comes to mind is a classmate from my first year of university named An.
At first, I didn't have a very good impression of him. He often arrived late to class, interrupted others during group discussions, and came across as quite arrogant. Because of that, I found him a bit difficult to get along with.

At that time, we were randomly assigned to the same group for a presentation project. To be honest, I was quite concerned that he wouldn't contribute much. However, as we started working together, my opinion of him gradually changed. He turned out to be surprisingly responsible and creative. He came up with several great ideas and even stayed up late to help us edit our slides and practice the rehearsal.
In the end, we delivered the presentation smoothly, and our lecturer was very pleased with the final result, so we ended up getting a high score, which was really rewarding.

I later realised that his earlier behavior wasn't because he was rude, but more because he was a bit socially awkward and didn't know how to express himself properly.

After the project, we started talking more and became much closer. He actually became one of the most reliable and supportive friends I had at university.

This experience taught me an important lesson: not to judge people too quickly based on first impressions. Sometimes, people just need time to show their true personality, and I'm really glad I gave him a second chance.
